00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:03.880
Let's go next to our caller from the nine
five, four area code.

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:06.650
Who's calling today from nine five, four.

00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:07.650
Hello.

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:09.410
Yeah, that's true.

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:12.120
Hey David, how are you man?

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:16.289
Uh, although I'm right wing, I have watched
your show for a couple months now and uh,

00:00:16.289 --> 00:00:22.030
honestly just want to say, I really appreciate
you being, uh, as genuine as you can and you

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:28.859
know, hopefully not to bias, but, uh, my question
is more on a personal know David.

00:00:28.859 --> 00:00:35.920
I'm in college right now and I'd been thinking
of, you know, uh, majoring in political science,

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:41.340
but I'm kind of naive when it comes to the
opportunities that may lead with that.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:44.820
Uh, any, any information you may give me or,

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:47.379
well, listen here, here's my view.

00:00:47.379 --> 00:00:54.159
I think that college basically is primarily
for in a lot of different majors.

00:00:54.159 --> 00:01:00.969
It is to learn how to think, learn how to
learn, learn how to exist in, uh, environments

00:01:00.969 --> 00:01:05.830
that are different than just with family and
friends and starting to learn, you know, behaving

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:09.920
diplomatically and managing relationships
and these types of things.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:18.570
And also I wish that maybe things were different,
but so much of what exists in terms of opportunities

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:23.511
is not so much about like the subject matter
that you memorize and learn in college, but

00:01:23.511 --> 00:01:28.160
about understanding that this is a world that
depends on relationships and networking and

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:29.290
who, you know.

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:34.150
So for me, you know, if the idea is to major
in political science and get an internship

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:39.270
with an elected official and use that to maybe
become an aid to a polo, you know, the, there's

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:41.240
a sort of a path there.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:46.060
But for me, mostly political sciences, like,
listen, you could do poly Psi, you could do

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:50.560
philosophy, you could do psychology, you could
do sociology, you could do economics.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:54.940
Or a lot of these are understanding how to
think and learn.

00:01:54.940 --> 00:02:00.020
And a lot of the job opportunities will be
very similar no matter where you get a four

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:05.979
year degree for no matter which s, um, a major
you have for your four year degree and it's

00:02:05.979 --> 00:02:08.940
mostly going to come down to, are you good
at building relationships?

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:13.280
Are you a go getter in terms of getting internships
and talking to people?

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.709
So it's really like if you're interested in
it, do it, but you really need to think about

00:02:16.709 --> 00:02:20.430
what it is you want to be doing when you're,
when you're out of college.

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:21.430
Wow.

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:22.569
What a great answer, David.

00:02:22.569 --> 00:02:24.159
I appreciate it.

00:02:24.159 --> 00:02:29.170
